#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand snap security policies and interfaces, including application "
"confinement, resource access controls, and protection for users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:8
msgid "Security policies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:10
msgid ""
"Without {ref}`custom flags at installation <interfaces-install-modes>`, or "
"subsequent {ref}`interface connections <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-"
"connect-interfaces>`, snaps remain confined to a restrictive security "
"sandbox, preventing access to system resources outside the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:12
msgid ""
"Snap developers need to be aware which system resources their applications "
"depend on from within the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:14
msgid ""
"Security policies and store policies work together to allow developers to "
"quickly confine and update their applications to provide safety to end "
"users. This document provides an overview of core mechanisms that underpins "
"the secure snap sandbox as well as information on how to configure and work "
"with the security policies for snaps you publish."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:16
msgid ""
"For more general details on what confinement entails, see {ref}`Snap "
"confinement <explanation-security-snap-confinement>`, and see below for "
"implementation details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:18
msgid "Security overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:20
msgid ""
"Application developers should not need to know about, or understand, the low-"
"level implementation details of how a security policy is enforced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:22
msgid ""
"Each command declared with the `apps` {ref}`snap metadata <reference-"
"development-yaml-schemas-the-snap-format>` is tracked by the system "
"assigning a security label to the command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:24
msgid ""
"This security label takes the form of `snap.<snap>.<app>` where `<snap>` is "
"the name of the snap, and `<app>` is the application name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:26
msgid "For example, the following is an app declaration from `snap.yaml`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:28
msgid ""
"name: foo\n"
"version: 1.0\n"
"apps:\n"
"  bar:\n"
"    command: bar\n"
"  baz:\n"
"    command: baz\n"
"    daemon: simple\n"
"    plugs: [network]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:40
msgid ""
"the security label for  `bar`  is  `snap.foo.bar` . It uses only the default "
"policy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:41
msgid ""
"the security label for  `baz`  is  `snap.foo.baz` . It uses the  `default`  "
"policy plus the  `network`  interface security policy as provided by the "
"core snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:43
msgid ""
"This security label is used throughout the system, including during the "
"process confinement phase when running the application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:45
msgid "Under the hood, the application runner does the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:47
msgid "Sets up various environment variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:48
msgid "`HOME`: set to `SNAP_USER_DATA` for all commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:49
msgid "`SNAP`: read-only install directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:50
msgid ""
"`SNAP_ARCH`: the architecture of device (eg, amd64, arm64, armhf, i386, etc)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:51
msgid "`SNAP_DATA`: writable area for a particular revision of the snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:52
msgid "`SNAP_COMMON`: writable area common across all revisions of the snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:53
msgid ""
"`SNAP_LIBRARY_PATH`: additional directories which should be added to "
"`LD_LIBRARY_PATH`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:54
msgid "`SNAP_NAME`: snap name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:55
msgid ""
"`SNAP_INSTANCE_NAME`: snap instance name incl. instance key if one is set "
"(snapd 2.36+)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:56
msgid "`SNAP_INSTANCE_KEY`: instance key if any (snapd 2.36+)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:57
msgid "`SNAP_REVISION`: store revision of the snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:58
msgid ""
"`SNAP_USER_DATA`: per-user writable area for a particular revision of the "
"snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:59
msgid ""
"`SNAP_USER_COMMON`: per-user writable area common across all revisions of "
"the snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:60
msgid "`SNAP_VERSION`: snap version (from `snap.yaml`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:62
msgid ""
"When hardware is assigned to the snap, a device cgroup is set up with "
"default devices (eg, /dev/null, /dev/urandom, etc) and any devices that are "
"assigned to this snap. Hardware is assigned with _interface_ connections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:64
msgid ""
"Sets up a private mount namespace shared across all the commands in the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:66
msgid ""
"Sets up a private `/tmp` directory using a per-snap private mount namespace "
"and mounting a per-snap directory on /tmp."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:68
msgid "Sets up a new instance of _devpts_ per command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:70
msgid "Sets up the seccomp filter for the command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:72
msgid ""
"Executes the command under the command-specific AppArmor profile under a "
"default nice value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:74
msgid "Security building blocks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:76
msgid "Cryptography"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:78
msgid ""
"The snap packaging system employs various cryptographic technologies to "
"secure local and remote snap operations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:80
msgid ""
"Locally, these are handled by the snap daemon, *snapd*, while remote "
"connections require mediation between snapd and the {ref}`Snap Store <ref-"
"glossary_snap-store>`, which supports its own set of cryptographic "
"technologies. Both sets of these are listed below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:82
msgid "Snapd (snap daemon) cryptography"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:84
msgid ""
"**Digital signatures for [assertions](https://ubuntu.com/core/docs/reference/"
"assertions)** </br>[SHA3-384] and [SHA512] for hashing, [OpenGPG signature "
"packet] V4 signatures with [RSA] 4096/8192 keys."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:84
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:86
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:88
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:90
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:92
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:105
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:107
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:109
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:111
#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:113
msgid "</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:86
msgid "**Hashing of snaps**</br> [SHA3-384]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:88
msgid ""
"**HTTPS communication**</br> Snapd uses the [Go standard library TLS "
"package] for the client, configured to use TLS 1.2 or later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:90
msgid ""
"**Device session request signing**</br> Same as digital signatures for "
"assertions: [SHA3-384], [SHA512] and [OpenGPG signature packet]. [RSA] 4096 "
"device key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:92
msgid ""
"**Macaroons for authorisation and authentication**</br> Snapd uses them via "
"[Go macaroon V1], which means [SHA256] [HMAC]s and [NaCL secretbox]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:95
msgid ""
"In the standard snapd, cryptographic functionality is provided by Go’s "
"native cryptographic implementations from the Go standard library. These "
"implementations may make use of architecture-specific optimizations (CPU "
"instruction set extensions such as AES-NI or SHA) where available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:97
msgid ""
"Snapd is also being developed in a FIPS variant - not yet generally "
"available - in which cryptographic operations are provided by OpenSSL rather "
"than Go’s native implementations. In this variant, OpenSSL may either be "
"bundled with snapd or supplied by the host system, enabling integration with "
"cryptographic modules validated under FIPS in environments that require FIPS "
"compliance. For more information about FIPS see: [Information about FIPS on "
"Ubuntu 22.04]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:99
msgid ""
"In both variants, cryptographically secure randomness is sourced from the "
"operating system via the Linux kernel’s randomness interfaces (for example "
"getrandom(2)), which provide entropy to user-space cryptographic libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:103
msgid "Snap Store cryptography"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:105
msgid ""
"**Digital signatures for [assertions](https://ubuntu.com/core/docs/reference/"
"assertions)**</br> The key ID of the signing key is encoded with [SHA3-384], "
"and the assertion is signed with either 4096-bit or 8192-bit [RSA]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:107
msgid ""
"**Hashing of artifacts**</br> The store generates many hashes of an uploaded "
"artefact using [SHA3-384], [SHA256] and [SHA512] to ensure the uniqueness "
"and integrity of the artefact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:109
msgid ""
"**Macaroons for authorisation and authentication**</br> [SHA256] [HMAC] and "
"[NaCL secretbox]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:111
msgid ""
"**Macaroons encryption of 3rd party caveats**</br> Salsa20 ([NaCL secretbox])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:113
msgid ""
"**Enterprise Store nonce signing**</br>  Used as additional security for "
"REST API access. [RSA] 4096-bit is used to sign and verify the nonce."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:126
msgid "Confinement and isolation mechanisms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:128
msgid ""
"When a snap is installed, its metadata is examined and is used to derive "
"**AppArmor** profiles, **Seccomp** filters and device **cgroup** rules, "
"alongside **traditional permissions**. This combination provides strong "
"application confinement and isolation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:130
msgid "AppArmor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:132
msgid ""
"AppArmor profiles are generated for each command. These have the appropriate "
"security label and command-specific AppArmor rules to mediate file access, "
"application execution, Linux capabilities, mount, ptrace, IPC, signals, "
"coarse-grained networking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:134
msgid ""
"As already mentioned, each command runs under an app-specific default policy "
"that may be extended through declared interfaces which are expressed in the "
"metadata as `plugs` and `slots`. AppArmor policy violations in strict mode "
"snaps will be denied access, and typically have errno set to `EACCES`. The "
"violation will typically be logged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:136
msgid ""
"See {ref}`AppArmor violations <how-to-guides-fix-common-issues-debug-snaps>` "
"for help tracking AppArmor problems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:138
msgid "Seccomp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:140
msgid ""
"A seccomp filter is generated for each command in a snap to run under, "
"enabling allowlist syscall filtering, which can then be extended through "
"declared interfaces expressed in the metadata as `plugs` and `slots`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:142
msgid ""
"Processes with seccomp policy violations will be denied access to the system "
"call with errno set to `EPERM` (snapd releases prior to 2.32 receive "
"`SIGSYS`) and the violation is logged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:144
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Seccomp violations <explanation-security-security-policies>` for "
"help tracking Seccomp problems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:146
msgid "Device cgroup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:148
msgid ""
"udev rules are generated for each command to tag devices so they may be "
"added/removed to the command's device cgroup. By default, however, no "
"devices are tagged and the device cgroup is not used, with AppArmor used to "
"mediate access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:150
msgid ""
"As determined by snapd, a device cgroup may be used in addition to AppArmor "
"when a dependent interface is declared, as expressed through `plugs` and "
"`slots` in the metadata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:152
msgid ""
"Processes accessing devices not in the snap-specific device cgroup will be "
"denied access with errno set to `EPERM`. Access violations are not logged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:154
msgid "Traditional permissions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:156
msgid ""
"Traditional file permissions (owner, group, file ACLs and others) are also "
"enforced with snaps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:158
msgid ""
"Processes trying to access resources which the traditional file permissions "
"do not allow are denied access with errno typically set to `EACCES` (see the "
"man page for the operation for specifics). Access violations are not logged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:160
msgid "Security policy implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:162
msgid ""
"The snap daemon uses AppArmor and Seccomp to create a security policy that "
"is linked to a specific snap revision. This governs what a snap can access "
"on your system. AppArmor profiles and Seccomp filters are created for each "
"command, and while AppArmor profiles can be changed and reloaded while a "
"process is running, Seccomp filters cannot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:164
msgid "Interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:166
msgid ""
"Interfaces are implemented as plugs and slots. A plug in one snap may "
"connect to a slot in another and this provides access to the resources "
"required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:168
msgid ""
"The _snap connections_ command can be used to see available interfaces "
"alongside their slots and plugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:170
msgid ""
"$ snap connections\n"
"Interface           Plug                                Slot               "
"Notes\n"
"home                wormhole:home                       :home              "
"-\n"
"log-observe         gnome-logs:log-observe              :log-observe       "
"-\n"
"mount-observe       gnome-system-monitor:mount-observe  :mount-observe     "
"-\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:179
msgid ""
"In the above example output, the  `gnome-logs`  snap is connected to the "
"`log-observe`  interface, which means the security policy from  `log-"
"observe`  has been added to `gnome-logs`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:181
msgid "Interfaces can be declared either per-snap or per-command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:182
msgid ""
"if declared per-snap, all the commands within the snap have the interface "
"security policy added to each command’s security policy when the interface "
"is connected"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:183
msgid ""
"if declared per-command, only the commands within the snap that declare use "
"of the interface have the specified interface security policy added to them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:185
msgid ""
"An interface may either auto-connect upon install, or require the user to "
"manually connect them. Interface connections and disconnections are "
"performed via the  `snap connect`  and  `snap disconnect` commands. See {ref}"
"`interfaces <explanation-interfaces-all-about-interfaces>` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:187
msgid "Policy switch during refresh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:189
msgid ""
"Snap security policy permits read and write access for the current revision, "
"and read-only access for other revisions to preserve the capabilities of "
"_snap revert_. More specifically, if `123` is the current revision, AppArmor "
"policy is set to allow `rw` on `SNAP_DATA=/var/snap/foo/123`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:191
msgid ""
"Before _refresh awareness_ became available, if a refresh occurred while a "
"snap was running, its AppArmor policy would be updated to allow `w` (write) "
"on the new version and `r` (read) on the older versions, including the "
"running version. The policy was applied immediately, which meant that write "
"operations would start to fail for running processes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:194
msgid "Refresh awareness"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:196
msgid ""
"By default, a service running from a snap needs to be restarted whenever the "
"snap is refreshed (see {ref}`Services and daemons <explanation-security-"
"security-policies>` for more details)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:198
msgid ""
"Stopping and starting a service is a requirement to support {ref}`snap "
"revert <explanation-security-security-policies>` and its copying of a snap’s "
"system data from the current version to the new version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:200
msgid ""
"System data typically includes databases, data files, and configuration "
"files (see {ref}`Data locations <interfaces-data-locations>`), although all "
"of this is up to the snap developer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:202
msgid ""
"Reverting a snap with `snap revert` restores a snap’s system data to its "
"prior state, and services accessing this data need to be stopped to protect "
"the integrity of the data and also to facilitate changes to security policy "
"that are required when a snap updates its system data location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:204
msgid ""
"To help mitigate any potential issues when a restart is required, _snapd_ "
"will check for running processes associated with the snap before each "
"refresh:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:206
msgid "if **no processes are running**, the refresh is performed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:207
msgid ""
"if **systemd-initiated processes** are detected, their associated units are "
"first stopped, the snap refreshed, and those units started again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:208
msgid ""
"if other **snap-initiated processes** are detected, {ref}`refresh awareness "
"<explanation-how-snaps-work-refresh-awareness>` is used to mediate the "
"update."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:210
msgid "Information security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:211
msgid ""
"Snapd includes the following built-in features that interacts with user "
"information:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:213
msgid ""
"{ref}`System configuration options <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-set-system-"
"options>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:214
msgid ""
"{ref}`Snap specific configurations options <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-"
"configure-snaps>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:215
msgid ""
"{ref}`Snapshots <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-create-data-snapshots>` of snap "
"{ref}`user data <interfaces-data-locations>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:216
msgid ""
"The {ref}`home <interfaces-home-interface>` interface allows access to non-"
"hidden files in the user’s home"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:217
msgid ""
"The {ref}`personal-files <interfaces-personal-files-interface>` interface "
"allows access to specified files in the user's home"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:218
msgid ""
"{ref}`Persisted data on Ubuntu Core devices <interfaces-data-"
"locations_ubuntu-core-persisted-data>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:220
msgid ""
"Snapd is designed to make these interactions secure by default. Developers "
"are expected to implement their own data storage solutions, e.g. database "
"and configuration files, on top of snapd’s secure mechanisms. This means "
"developers share responsibility for ensuring the availability, integrity, "
"confidentiality, and compatibility of user data over the lifetime of a snap "
"or snap-based product."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:222
msgid ""
"Snaps that use classic confinement do not benefit from snapd's sandboxing "
"and isolation features, and thus operate with fewer security boundaries. "
"This elevated level of access introduces a higher risk to the "
"confidentiality and integrity of user data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:224
msgid ""
"To mitigate this risk, snapd enforces an explicit acknowledgment from users "
"by requiring the `--classic` flag at install time. Furthermore, to protect "
"the ecosystem, only publishers who have passed a strict review and vetting "
"process are permitted to publish classic snaps in the Snap Store."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:226
msgid "How to establish strict confinement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:228
msgid ""
"The correct mindset is to request the minimum set of permissions necessary "
"for a snap to function as currently required. When multiple interfaces "
"provide overlapping permissions, choose the most restrictive one that still "
"satisfies the requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:230
msgid ""
"Avoid defining [top-level plugs](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/"
"stable/reference/project-file/snapcraft-yaml/) or slots that implicitly "
"apply an interface to all apps and daemons in the snap. Instead, declare "
"permissions as narrowly as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:232
msgid ""
"These guidelines are considered during store approval of permission "
"requests. Deviations from best practice will require a justification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:234
msgid ""
"Sometimes it is also required to extend what is allowed by an existing "
"interface or even to implement a new interface. When in doubt, feel free to "
"consult the [snapd team on the snapcraft forum](https://forum.snapcraft.io/c/"
"snapd/5)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:236
msgid ""
"[How to create a simple confined snap](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"snapcraft/stable/how-to/crafting/enable-classic-confinement)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:237
msgid ""
"{ref}`How to identify which interfaces are required to solve policy "
"violations <how-to-guides-fix-common-issues-debug-snaps>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:238
msgid ""
"{ref}`AppArmor violations <how-to-guides-fix-common-issues-debug-snaps>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:239
msgid "{ref}`Seccomp violations <how-to-guides-fix-common-issues-debug-snaps>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:240
msgid ""
"{ref}`File permissions and cgroups <explanation-security-security-policies>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:241
msgid ""
"{ref}`How to request store approval for snap policy changes <interfaces-"
"reviewing-classic-confinement-snaps>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:243
msgid ""
"To publish a classic snap that operates outside these restrictions, you must "
"submit a classic confinement request that involves a rigorous process for "
"vetting the publisher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:245
msgid "Security maintenance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:247
msgid ""
"Snapd is delivered both as Debian packages and as snaps, and the security "
"maintenance responsibilities and lifecycles differ depending on the delivery "
"mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:249
msgid ""
"The snapd Debian packages are maintained by the Ubuntu Security Team and "
"receive security updates through the standard Ubuntu security maintenance "
"process. The duration of this maintenance follows the [Ubuntu Release "
"Cycle]. This lifecycle applies only to the snapd deb packages provided as "
"part of Ubuntu releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:251
msgid ""
"The snapd snap is maintained by the snapd team, who are responsible for "
"delivering security updates through snap revisions. The lifecycle and "
"maintenance model for snapd snaps is separate from the Ubuntu release "
"lifecycle and follows the Ubuntu Core and snap track maintenance model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:253
msgid ""
"Ubuntu Core 16 includes snapd embedded within the core snap (which functions "
"as the base operating system snap). Security maintenance for snapd in the "
"Ubuntu Core 16 core snap is handled by the snapd team through updates to the "
"core snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:257
msgid "Security updates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:259
msgid ""
"The snapd team allocates capacity for security fixes following each major "
"snapd release. Major snapd releases currently occur on an approximately two-"
"month cadence, and a planned security update window is scheduled after each "
"major release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:261
msgid ""
"These security update releases are primarily intended to address high-"
"priority security vulnerabilities that require timely remediation outside of "
"the normal feature release cycle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:263
msgid ""
"Lower-priority security vulnerabilities may be accumulated and delivered as "
"part of these planned security update releases. Security updates may be "
"released outside of this preferred cadence where required to address "
"critical vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:265
msgid ""
"Security vulnerabilities are handled under a responsible disclosure process. "
"Security issues are tracked and developed privately and are not publicly "
"visible until fixes are released and the vulnerability is disclosed. This "
"ensures that users receive fixes before detailed vulnerability information "
"becomes publicly available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:268
msgid "How to report vulnerabilities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/security/security-policies.md:270
msgid ""
"Please report [suspected security vulnerabilities] **privately** by "
"following the instructions in [SECURITY.md]."
msgstr ""
